# Line endings are pinned here because this repository is built, tested and
# shipped on macOS/Linux (CI, the release scripts, the Pi recipe) and is also
# checked out on Windows. Git for Windows' installer defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites text files to CRLF IN THE WORKING TREE:
# the shell scripts then start with "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r" and fail in Linux
# with exit 127 ("bad interpreter"), and gofmt -l flags nearly every .go file
# because it sees a trailing CR on every line. The committed blobs are LF, so
# the failure only appears on that checkout - which is exactly the kind of
# machine-specific breakage a repository can pin away (audit L-1).
#
# `text=auto` still detects binary files and leaves their bytes alone; the
# explicit `eol=lf` is what makes the CHECKOUT match the blob on every host,
# whatever the host's own autocrlf setting is.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The scripts are executed directly (./scripts/...), so the shebang must carry
# no trailing CR on any platform. Stated separately from the rule above because
# it is the failure an operator actually hit, and a rule that names it is the
# one a future contributor will find.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Nothing else needs pinning: every other tracked file is text (Go, Markdown,
# Makefile, JSON genesis/committee files, workflow YAML), and there are no
# tracked binaries - the one .exe in the tree is git-ignored build output, and
# `dist/` artifacts are never committed. If a binary is ever checked in, give it
# `binary` here before it is added, or text=auto's heuristic is all that
# protects it.
